A||||20||fiero56||bad clutch already?!?!||||9||
Z||000000||fiero56||01-26-2000||04:27 PM||||While driving home from work this afternoon(in my '88 Formula v-6 5speed), I noticed that my brand new clutch(under 2,000 miles) was slipping. I was driving up a fairly steep hill at 35mph(3rd gear, 2,100rpm) and if I stepped on the gas, maybe half way, the rpms would jump up(3,500rpm), but speed stayed constant. After I let off, I could feel the clutch grab again. <BR>I think I know why. The list of problems and headaches it has caused me continues to increase in length. I hope this is the last problem.(If you want a list of problems caused by my using ATF e-mail me)<BR>I used ATF in my clutch system. The seals expaned so much in the cylinders, most of the spring pressure from the p. plate was used up forcing the seals back out. It wasn't able to put enough pressure on the clutch disk itself, therefore allowing the disk to slip continuously, unnoticed. Now, after putting dot3 back in, the plate is able to put proper pressure on the disk, but the disk is worn out from the constant slipping when using ATF. I just want to add, $^%$@^&@$%%&^*^%$#%^&Q, ATF never again!!!<P>Any comments/disagreements on my hypothesis? <P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||fiero56||01-26-2000||05:32 PM||||I just got back from a diagnosis run. The car runs strong through 1st and 2nd gears. 3rd will slip under heavy acceleration and higher rpms, around 2,800. 4th slips easily anywhere above 2,100 rpm. 5th, don't know, rush hour, couldn't get it to slip, but I only got up to 50mph. Is a worn clutch easier to get to slip in higher gears than the lower ones?<P>I would like to add, I noticed yesterday after driving up a very long very steep hill on the highway, while pressing down the clutch pedal on the exit ramp, my pedal kinda' vibrated like the flywheel was warped, indicating 5th maybe slips under haevier load at higher rpms.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-26-2000||06:03 PM||||56,<P>You are obviously a much better mechanic than I when it comes to clutches... but are you sure that your pedal is adjusted properly.  One time with my truck, I had adjusted the clutch pedal and thought that it drove fine.  When I took it down to E-check (Don't you just love those idiots!), the guy did not know how to drive a manual and didn't bother to tell me before he tried.  When I got out of the booth, all I could smell was burnt clutch.  I got back in to drive off and didn't get 100 yards before B O O M... no clutch!  There was actually a big sound, I thought that I had catastrophic mechanical damage.  I called up my cousin (X-mechanic) and he started looking at things.  Amazingly it was only that the linkage had become severly misadjusted.  Mind you that your Fiero is a nice machine and my truck... well we will just say that it was not to be easy on it.  It is a far fetched idea, but I would double check the pedal and associated linkage before tearing into the system again.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||fiero56||01-26-2000||06:08 PM||||William,<BR>The only linkage that deals with the Fiero transmission, is the shift linkage(shift and select cables). The clutch on a Fiero is hydraulic which means there is no adjustment needed. I assume your truck used a cable that was pulled by pushing down the pedal. Unless I am wrong(would be nice) I don't think there is anything I can adjust that would stop the clutch slippage. If I read your post wrong, let me know. You may be speaking of something that I don't know about. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-26-2000||06:14 PM||||Will3, the Fiero clutch has no adjustment at all.<P>F56, That could be the case with the slave binding. could also be the fork and/or throwout binding making it even worse make sure you check those when it's apart. likely the stupid thing glazed up on you, but you may also have a weak pressure plate. You did replace the pressure plat when you did the clutch? Was it a new one or a referb?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-26-2000||06:26 PM||||Ogre,<BR>I won't touch anything refurbished(if I can afford not to). I replaced master, slave, t.o. bearing, clutch disk, and pressure plate. All are/were brand new. Fork moved freely. Its sounding like cradle dropping time.<BR>The current slave doesn't bind, has only a few hundred miles on it(I went out and checked). I replaced both master and slave cylinders with brand new after the ATF problems. Its running DOT3 right now and forever!<P>As the simpsons would put it, "now close ya' eyes, and drop ye' briches.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||01-26-2000||06:53 PM||||56,<P>It is amazing that what is in my head just doesn't find its way to the keyboard sometimes.  I do realize that the Fiero has a hyd. clutch system.  I meant to make the point to check your slave cyl. travel etc. before replacing your clutch, but instead it came out "linkage".  I do remember that there was some discussion about Ed Parks reccomendation to not surface the flywheel in your case.  Do you think that flywheel warpage could be causing your problem?  Another longshot, but an idea none the less.  You should be able to tell based on wear marks once you get inside/under there though.<P>Good Luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000007||fiero56||01-26-2000||06:58 PM||||I've thought about the flywheel warpage thing too. It is barly noticeable, so I'm not sure if it is causing this as well or not. I think this time I will replace it as well. Anyone know where you can get a brand new flywheel for the '88? They are not compatible with the '84-'87.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||08:51 PM||||NOT to resurface the flywheel?<P>If what is reported is true and the clutch is glazed then WHY? The flywheel is probably as blue as a smurf from over heating. Warped flywheel, if it is something else I would be intrigued as to what else creates a vibration in the clutch pedal. I have not understood this ATF recomendation from the start. I feel for you having to go back in, it totally sucks to pull a tranny over again. <P>Please let us know what the results are! I would predict not only that the flywheel is warped and blue but you might even find surface fractures. Hope Im wrong and you find something as easy as a defective clutch bearing...||||reg||9
Z||000009||fiero56||01-26-2000||09:26 PM||||Back when I was getting ready to tear this thing apart the first time I asked Ed Parks(<B>the</B> Fiero authority) what to do with the flywheel. He said if it is flat and in good shape leave it be. If it is warped buy a new one. I was told the shops that do this work never really look to see how much they take off, and it could be a headache. When I did the first job, the flywheel looked almost brand new, so I left it alone. I think it is warped now due to the constantly slipping clutch.<P>I have just spent the better half of this afternoon calling all over town and the country, looking to see how fast I can get parts in. I want to the repair this weekend. Do you have any idea how hard it is to get parts for a Fiero in the Dayton area?! You are right, this does royaly suck. I was just in the innards of my car for two weeks straight just a few months ago. Here it comes again. ATF sucks, don't touch the $ht!!! I did and here is the cost thus far, leaving out my future clutch cost. $300!!! And now another clutch set and flywheel, I don't even want to know!<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000010||David Gamache||01-26-2000||09:27 PM||||Ok, here goes. I will relate as much as I remember about clutch problems. Slave cylinders when replaced do not always travel quite as far as the orginal. It took 3 slave cylinders before one worked properly on one of my cars(the one that worked looked like the orginal and was purchased at pep boys). Bleed the slave properly(best to have a friend help). Faulty slave feels like a wasted clutch. <P>When replacing the disc,bearing and pressure plate it is best to have the fly re-surfaced like when brake pads are replaced. Glazing can cause slippage and vibration(locally in so cal a place called clutch masters provides the best service. They supply the dealer,do resurface work, carry all clutch parts and even flywheels. Best of all they are reasonable).<P>There is no adjustment for the clutch so cables are out. <P>Do not slow the car down using the clutch,use the brakes. My wife used to down shift like Mario and smoked a new clutch in a short time. In fact when the disc was removed the springs were falling out from excessive vibration,something I had never seen before. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||01-26-2000||10:02 PM||||56,<P>If you don't get parts this weekend (I already have plans for this weekend)... give me a call.  I would more than happy to donate an extra pair of hands for this task.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>937-259-5295 (work)||||reg||14
Z||000012||fiero56||01-26-2000||11:02 PM||||Thanks for the offer William. I hope to get everything I need overnighted. That way I can start late friday night and go nonstop through the weekend until I'm finished(all I need is coffee). Coffee and no sleep, I have very little time/patience to screw around with what ATF has done to my car. Plus the 2/5 trip is next weekend. No way am I missing that, I missed one already, not another.  I'll let you know if I have any problems. I don't think so, since I have every tool I need, and I have the procedure almost memorized.||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||11:49 PM||||I am afraid that I would have to disagree with THE Ed Parks. For something that is so hard to get to like the flywheel fo a Fiero, I would not want to take chances by "eyeballing it." Just like resurfacing brakes, its just piece of mind. If you have to worry about the machine shop taking too much off than find a competent machine shop. <P>As far as ED Parks being THE Fiero Authority, I would like to eventually meet him someday. Until then I would have to defer to my Fiero guru Keith "Fiero Doc" Huff.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||01-27-2000||12:01 AM||||Everyone is entitled to their opinions. Thats fine with me. Me, I don't even mess with resurfacing. When I replace pads, I buy rotors to. I don't have stuff resurfaced, turned, or rebuilt. I buy brand new, or forget it. Just the way I am. I am in the very early planning stage for an engine rebuild/upgrade right now, I don't like the thought of using anything over. It has to be brand new, or I don't like the thought of using it. I just recently bought a rebuilt brake caliper. Didn't want to, but I did. I'll see how it does.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mwbackus||01-27-2000||12:58 AM||||Sorry to hear of you misfortune 56, that kind of thing really burns me.  You may want to go with the 3.4 TDC flywheel, it weighs a little bit more, but they are much cheaper and work just fine.  <P>If all else fails in your search for clutch parts, try the Fiero Store.  They sell the RAM HD clutch, and offer a Kevlar disc if desired.  They should be able to over night one for ya.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||01-27-2000||10:04 AM||||Backus, I bought my first clutch set(hd) from the Fiero store. I was able to find a flywheel. I had to go to Pontiac for it, it's coming from Louisville and will be here tommorrow by 10am. $235. Another clutch set from the Fiero store. I talked to the guy and he gave me a slight break on the price. 2nd day priority mail is $43. I'm looking at around $474 this time. Cluth will be at my door by 10am Saturday morning. I figure if I work through the night on Friday, I will be ready for the parts Saturday morning anyway. Hope this taked care of it.<P>On the glazing note: I think the clutch would slip in any gear if the flywheel were glazed. Not just 3rd, 4th, and maybe 5th.||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||01-27-2000||07:02 PM||||Actually I meant that the clutch disk is glazed......<P>The higher gears actually put more resistance to the clutch. It's common to see a clutch slip in high gears first in regular driving.<P>If the old flywheel was warped then that could have helped speed up a failure. I'd be very suprised if it's not warped now.||||reg||1
Z||000018||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||07:25 PM||||'56,<P>My weekend plans just fell through.  Let me know if you need any help... give me a call at the office tomorrow (937-259-5295).  If you would rather do it yourself.. that it fine too.  Just would like to help out a guy in dire straights. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000019||fiero56||01-27-2000||10:15 PM||||Sorry Ogre, looks like misunderstood your post. The disk could be glazed. The flywheel wasn't warped(noticeably in the pedal) until just last week. Since the clutch slips pretty easily now, I too would be surprised if the flywheel weren't warped.<P>William, I've got my dad helping with this too, again. If I do need any help, I'll be sure to call you. I hope to have this thing pretty well wrapped up by late Saturday. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||fieroshop||01-28-2000||01:26 AM||||I will say one thing. Since I have a clutch that is manufactured expressly for my shop and it carries a double your previous miles on your clutch warranty. But unless you resurface or replace the flywheel the warranty is null and void. So I don't where you  guys think that not resufacing a flywheel you can get away with it. 99.9% of the time when you pull a clutch there are either hairline cracks that you can not see or hot spots or a combination of both. Just my professional opinion owing a Fiero Shop and having a special clutch built for me.||||reg||1
